# Everything this directory produces is production exercise text. The repo is public.
#
# **Deny everything, then name what may be committed.** This used to be the other way round — a list
# of the output names known at the time — and on 2026-08-31 that list lost. The release produced
# `out-final/`, `out-hidden/`, `out-wrapped/`, `payload-262.json`, `receipt-262.json` and a pile of
# `after-*.md`, none of which matched a rule: the file said `payload.jsonl` and the run wrote
# `payload.json`. Several hundred real exercises sat untracked in a public repository until someone
# noticed and excluded them in `.git/info/exclude`, which protects exactly one clone.
#
# An allowlist cannot lose that way. A new script inventing a new output name is ignored by default,
# and the cost of the inversion is that adding a *tracked* file means adding a line here — which is
# the rare case and the one worth making deliberate.
#
# `doc/core/tsl-migration/.gitignore` is the same rule for the same reason.
*

!.gitignore
!README.md
!RUNBOOK.md
!*.py
!*.sql

# Synthetic, written by hand, and the only exercise text in here that is safe to publish.
!fixtures/
!fixtures/**

# Directories have to be walked into before anything inside them can be un-ignored, and `tests/`
# holds only test code.
!tests/
!tests/**
